            <pre><code id="code-install" set:html={highlight(installCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>one instance, both contracts</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-wire">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-wire" set:html={highlight(wireSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="connect" aria-labelledby="connect-h">
          <h2 id="connect-h">Connect and drive</h2>
          <p>
            The SQLite connection lives on a worker isolate; the main isolate never blocks on I/O. Open a file (or run in-memory), drive it with the same verbs the query builder compiles to, back it up, then disconnect. Unexpected isolate death fails pending operations instead of hanging them.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>full lifecycle</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-use">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-use" set:html={highlight(useSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Aliases:</span> <code class="inline">connect()</code> and <code class="inline">disconnect()</code> also answer to <code class="inline">open()</code> and <code class="inline">close()</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="serialize" aria-labelledby="serialize-h">
          <h2 id="serialize-h">Serialized access</h2>
          <p>
            Concurrent callers queue behind a FIFO mutex in call order. Transactions (<code class="inline">BEGIN IMMEDIATE</code>, nested <code class="inline">SAVEPOINT</code>s) hold the connection exclusively, and concurrent transactions serialize atomically. Like the drift connection manager this mirrors, there is exactly one connection and it is never shared mid-statement.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="configure" aria-labelledby="configure-h">
          <h2 id="configure-h">Configure and export</h2>
          <p>Configuration is first-class, not a reopen dance:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>File path (exposed via <code class="inline">path</code> so the file can be exported by hand), in-memory mode, and read-only mode.</li>
            <li>Foreign keys, journal mode, synchronous mode, busy timeout, VFS, and URI filenames.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Export is two calls: <code class="inline">backupTo(path)</code> takes a hot backup via <code class="inline">VACUUM INTO</code> while connected; <code class="inline">restoreFrom(path)</code> is a cold file copy while disconnected.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="encrypt" aria-labelledby="encrypt-h">
          <h2 id="encrypt-h">Encrypted databases</h2>
          <p>
            Build the app with an encryption-capable SQLite (SQLite3MultipleCiphers is recommended; sqlcipher also works, since the driver only speaks <code class="inline">PRAGMA key</code>, which both understand). Declare it in the <strong>app's</strong> pubspec:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>pubspec.yaml, encryption capable</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-cipheryaml">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-cipheryaml" set:html={highlight(cipherYaml)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Then connect with a password the driver never retains:</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>connect with a key</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-cipher">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-cipher" set:html={highlight(cipherSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Prefer raw keys:</span> <code class="inline">SecurePassword.rawKey(bytes)</code> (32 random bytes) hex-encodes straight from bytes and never materializes a <code class="inline">String</code>. A wrong key surfaces as <code class="inline">SqlCipherAuthException</code>. See the <code class="inline">SecurePassword</code> docs for the GC-caveat details.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
          <h2 id="limits-h">Platform limits</h2>
          <p>Three hard edges, stated up front:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>VM-only.</strong> The driver uses <code class="inline">dart:io</code>, <code class="inline">dart:isolate</code>, and FFI. There is no web support.</li>
            <li><strong>Six value types cross the isolate boundary:</strong> null, bool, int, double, String, and Uint8List. Anything else throws <code class="inline">ArgumentError</code> before reaching SQLite.</li>
            <li><strong>Typed failures.</strong> SQLite errors surface as <code class="inline">SqliteDriverException</code> with the extended result code; calls while disconnected throw <code class="inline">SqliteDriverClosedException</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
          <h2 id="skills-h">Agent skills</h2>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">sqlite3_driver_forge</code> also ships its
            guidance as agent skills: prescriptive rules and examples authored
            for Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot, Cline, and any other agent working
            in your repository. Each skill is a <code class="inline">SKILL.md</code>
            shipped inside the package, so the advice is versioned with the API
            you actually installed.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>install the package skills</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-skills">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-skills" set:html={highlight(skillsCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Two skills ship with <code class="inline">sqlite3_driver_forge</code>:</p>
          <table class="scope">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite3-driver-forge-driver</th><td>SqliteDriver lifecycle: config, query/execute/insert, transactions, backup and restore, error types.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite3-driver-forge-cipher</th><td>Encrypted databases: sqlite3mc hook setup, SecurePassword vs rawKey, connect and changePassword.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Where they land:</span> run the command in an app that already depends on <code class="inline">sqlite3_driver_forge</code>. The installer checks each directory's package prefix and writes the matching skills to your project's <code class="inline">.agents/skills/</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

